<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-functions-in-real-life" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Functions in Real Life</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.1 Functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                            <ul>
                              <li>Appreciate the use of functions in real life situations</li>
                              <li>Apply functions to real life situations</li>
                            </ul>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    15 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To let learners practise turning a real-life situation into an actual function rule, rather than only naming which quantity depends on which.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Work through the mango example with the whole class first, modelling how to choose letters for the input and output before writing the rule. Then let learners write the remaining three function rules in pairs. Circulate and check that each rule uses consistent notation, such as <m>C(k)</m> for cost in terms of kilograms.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>


    <!-- Add Activity -->

    <activity xml:id="activity-functions-real-life">
        <p>
            Functions are used every day to describe relationships between quantities. The value of one quantity often depends on another quantity.
        </p>
        <p>
            For the mango situation below, mangoes cost 20 shillings each. Let <m>k</m> be the number of kilograms bought and let <m>C</m> be the total cost in shillings.
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Mangoes cost 20 shillings per kilogram. Write a function <m>C(k)</m> for the total cost of buying <m>k</m> kilograms of mangoes.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A car travels at a constant speed of 60 kilometres per hour. Write a function <m>D(t)</m> for the distance travelled after <m>t</m> hours.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        An employee earns 150 shillings for every hour worked. Write a function <m>E(h)</m> for the total amount earned after <m>h</m> hours.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A cup of tea starts at 90 degrees Celsius and cools by 3 degrees every minute. Write a function <m>T(m)</m> for the temperature of the tea after <m>m</m> minutes.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
            Once you have written all four function rules, discuss the following questions.
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol marker="(i)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        In each function, which letter represents the input and which represents the output?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Use your function from part (a) to find the cost of buying 5 kilograms of mangoes. Check that your answer makes sense.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Why are functions useful for describing real-life situations like these?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-functions-real-life">
            <caption>Functions help describe relationships between quantities in daily life</caption>
            <image width="70%">
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]

                        \draw[rounded corners, thick] (0,0) rectangle (3,1);
                        \node at (1.5,0.5) {Input};

                        \draw[-&gt;, thick] (3,0.5)--(5,0.5);

                        \draw[rounded corners, thick] (5,0) rectangle (8,1);
                        \node at (6.5,0.5) {Function Rule};

                        \draw[-&gt;, thick] (8,0.5)--(10,0.5);

                        \draw[rounded corners, thick] (10,0) rectangle (13,1);
                        \node at (11.5,0.5) {Output};

                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </activity>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    15 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To connect the function rules learners wrote in the activity to the worked examples, and to introduce the idea of a practical domain.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways and Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Compare two or three pairs' function rules from the activity. Discuss any differences in notation and agree on a class standard, such as always naming the function after the output quantity.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Practical Domain:</alert> Use the worked examples to introduce the idea that not every real number makes sense as an input. For example, time and length cannot be negative. Ask learners to identify the practical domain for their own functions from the activity.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Inverse Functions in Context:</alert> Point out that several examples ask learners to find an inverse function, such as converting shillings back to dollars. Stress that the inverse function answers a different real-world question to the original function, even though the algebra is closely related.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

    <!-- <insight>
    </insight> -->

    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example xml:id="ex-escape-velocity">
        <statement>
            <p>
                To escape a planet's gravitational pull, a spacecraft must reach a specific escape velocity. This velocity <m>v</m> (in meters per second) is modeled as a function of the distance <m>r</m> from the planet's center by the formula:
                <md>
                    <mrow>v(r) = \sqrt{\frac{2GM}{r}}</mrow>
                </md>
                where <m>G</m> is the gravitational constant and <m>M</m> is the mass of the planet.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Identify the type of function represented by <m>v(r)</m> with respect to the variable <m>r</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Suppose for a specific celestial body, the constant value <m>2GM = 1.28 \times 10^{11}</m>. Calculate the escape velocity <m>v</m> when the spacecraft is at a distance of <m>r = 20,000</m> meters from the core.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The function relates the independent variable <m>r</m> in the denominator under a square root to the dependent variable <m>v</m>. This is a radical function combined with a rational relationship, demonstrating that escape velocity is inversely proportional to the square root of the distance.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            To find the velocity at <m>r = 20,000</m> meters, we substitute the given values into the function:
                            <md>
                                <mrow>v(20000) \amp= \sqrt{\frac{1.28 \times 10^{11}}{20000}}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= \sqrt{6400000}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= 2500</mrow>
                            </md>
                            The escape velocity at that distance is 2500 meters per second.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-time-dilation">
        <statement>
            <p>
                According to Einstein's theory of Special Relativity, time slows down for an object moving close to the speed of light. The dilated time <m>t_v</m> observed by a stationary onlooker is a function of the spaceship's velocity <m>v</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>t_v(v) = \frac{t_0}{\sqrt{1 - \frac{v^2}{c^2}}}</mrow>
                </md>
                where <m>t_0</m> is the proper time experienced on board the ship and <m>c</m> is the constant speed of light.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Describe what happens to the observed time <m>t_v</m> as the velocity <m>v</m> approaches the speed of light <m>c</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            An astronaut travels at a velocity of <m>v = 0.6c</m> for a journey that lasts <m>t_0 = 10</m> years according to their ship's clock. Find the time that has passed for observers back on Earth.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            As <m>v</m> gets closer to <m>c</m>, the ratio <m>\frac{v^2}{c^2}</m> approaches 1. This causes the value under the radical, <m>1 - \frac{v^2}{c^2}</m>, to approach 0. As the denominator drops toward zero, the total value of the function approaches infinity (<m>t_v \to \infty</m>).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            To calculate the Earth time when <m>t_0 = 10</m> and <m>v = 0.6c</m>, substitute these parameters into our function:
                            <md>
                                <mrow>t_v \amp= \frac{10}{\sqrt{1 - \frac{(0.6c)^2}{c^2}}}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= \frac{10}{\sqrt{1 - 0.36}}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= \frac{10}{\sqrt{0.64}}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= \frac{10}{0.8}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= 12.5</mrow>
                            </md>
                            Therefore, 12.5 years have elapsed for observers on Earth during the astronaut's 10-year trip.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-pendulum-period">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The time it takes for a simple pendulum to complete one full back-and-forth swing is called its period. The period <m>T</m> (in seconds) is a function of the pendulum's string length <m>L</m> (in meters), given by:
                <md>
                    <mrow>T(L) = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{L}{g}}</mrow>
                </md>
                where <m>g</m> represents the acceleration due to gravity.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            State the practical domain constraint for the length <m>L</m> in this physics application.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Assuming an environment where gravity <m>g = 9.8 \text{ m/s}^2</m>, calculate the exact period <m>T</m> of a pendulum that has a string length of <m>L = 9.8</m> meters.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            A pendulum string must have a physical, positive length to exist and swing freely. Mathematically, we also cannot take the square root of a negative value in real number functions. Thus, the practical domain constraint is <m>L \gt 0</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Substitute <m>g = 9.8</m> and <m>L = 9.8</m> into the period function:
                            <md>
                                <mrow>T(9.8) \amp= 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{9.8}{9.8}}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= 2\pi \sqrt{1}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= 2\pi</mrow>
                            </md>
                            The exact period of the pendulum is <m>2\pi</m> seconds (or approximately 6.28 seconds).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-keplers-third-law">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Kepler's Third Law describes the relationship between the distance of a planet from its host star and its orbital period. When normalized for our solar system using astronomical units (AU) and Earth years, the period <m>T</m> is expressed as a power function of the orbital radius <m>r</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>T(r) = r^{\frac{3}{2}}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Rewrite this power function using radical notation instead of a fractional exponent.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            An outer planet is discovered orbiting a distant star at an average distance of <m>4</m> AU. Determine its orbital period in years.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            A fractional exponent of <m>\frac{3}{2}</m> indicates a square root where the radicand is raised to the third power. The function can be written cleanly as:
                            <md>
                                <mrow>T(r) = \sqrt{r^3}</mrow>
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Substitute the orbital radius value of <m>r = 4</m> into the equation:
                            <md>
                                <mrow>T(4) \amp= 4^{\frac{3}{2}}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= (\sqrt{4})^3</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= 2^3</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= 8</mrow>
                            </md>
                            The planet takes exactly 8 Earth years to complete one full orbit around its star.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-assembly-size">
        <statement>
            <p>
                In political science, the "Cube Root Law" is an empirical model used to estimate the ideal size of a democratic country's national assembly. The assembly size <m>S</m> (number of seats) is modeled as a function of the total population <m>P</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>S(P) = P^{\frac{1}{3}}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Write down the name of this specific type of function.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            According to this model, what should the ideal size of a parliament be for a developing nation with a total population of <m>27,000,000</m> citizens?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            An equation with a fractional exponent of <m>\frac{1}{3}</m> maps an input to its cube root. This represents a cube root function, which is a type of radical function.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Substitute the population parameter <m>P = 27,000,000</m> directly into the function:
                            <md>
                                <mrow>S(27000000) \amp= (27,000,000)^{\frac{1}{3}}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= \sqrt[3]{27,000,000}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= 300</mrow>
                            </md>
                            Based on the political science model, the ideal size for the national assembly is exactly 300 seats.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

