    <!-- Add STACK exercises if made -->

    <!-- Add static exercises -->

    <exercises xml:id="ex-functions-real-life-1">
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A savings account starts with 1,500 shillings and increases by 250 shillings every month.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Write a function representing the amount saved after <m>m</m> months.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Find the amount saved after 6 months.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p><m>S(m)=1500+250m</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>3000</m></p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-functions-knec-mapping">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The Kenya National Examinations Council (KNEC) assigns a unique 11-digit index number to every student registering for the KCSE. Upon completion, students are awarded an aggregate grade ranging from A to E (a total of 12 distinct grades). Let set <m>S</m> represent the students, set <m>I</m> represent the index numbers, and set <m>G</m> represent the possible grades.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Does the mapping of index numbers to students (<m>I \to S</m>) represent a function? Classify it as one-to-one, many-to-one, or one-to-many.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Does the mapping of students to grades (<m>S \to G</m>) represent a function? Classify it.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If a university wants to map a specific grade to the students who achieved it (<m>G \to S</m>), would this relation be considered a mathematical function? Explain your reasoning.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Yes, it is a one-to-one function.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Yes, it is a many-to-one function.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>No, it is a one-to-many relation, not a function.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Every index number corresponds to exactly one unique student. Because no two students share the same index number, the relation is a one-to-one function.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Every student receives exactly one final KCSE grade, so it satisfies the definition of a function. However, thousands of students can receive the same grade (e.g., a B+), making it a many-to-one function.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Mapping a grade to students means one input (e.g., Grade A) maps to multiple outputs (all the students who scored an A). A valid function can only have one output for every input, so this is a one-to-many relation, not a function.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-functions-jamhuri-flare">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    During the Jamhuri Day celebrations at Nyayo Stadium, a flare is fired upwards. Its height <m>h</m> in meters above the ground after <m>t</m> seconds is modeled by the function:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>h(t) = -5t^2 + 40t + 2</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Calculate the maximum height the flare reaches.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Determine the practical domain and practical range for this function within the context of the real-world scenario. Give your answers to two decimal places where necessary.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p><m>82</m> meters</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Domain: <m>[0, 8.05]</m> seconds; Range: <m>[0, 82]</m> meters</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The maximum height occurs at the vertex of the parabola. We find the time at the vertex using <m>t = \frac{-b}{2a}</m>:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>t = \frac{-40}{2(-5)} = 4 \text{ seconds}</mrow>
                                </md>
                                Substitute <m>t = 4</m> into the equation to find the height:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>h(4) = -5(4)^2 + 40(4) + 2 = -80 + 160 + 2 = 82 \text{ meters}</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The practical domain is the time the flare is in the air, starting at <m>t = 0</m> until it hits the ground (<m>h(t) = 0</m>). Using the quadratic formula:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>t = \frac{-40 \pm \sqrt{1600 - 4(-5)(2)}}{-10} = \frac{-40 \pm \sqrt{1640}}{-10} \approx 8.05 \text{ seconds}</mrow>
                                </md>
                                Therefore, the practical domain is <m>[0, 8.05]</m>. The practical range spans from the ground level to the maximum height, which is <m>[0, 82]</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-functions-jkia-forex">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A forex bureau at Jomo Kenyatta International Airport converts US Dollars (USD) to Kenyan Shillings (KES). On a particular day, the amount of KES received, <m>K</m>, for exchanging <m>d</m> dollars is given by the function:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>K(d) = 135d - 300</mrow>
                    </md>
                    where 300 represents a fixed transaction fee in shillings.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Find the inverse function <m>d(K)</m> and explain what it represents in this context.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                A tourist is handed <m>26,700</m> KES after converting their money. Use the inverse function to determine how many USD they exchanged.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p><m>d(K) = \frac{K + 300}{135}</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>200</m> USD</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                To find the inverse function, we solve the equation for <m>d</m> in terms of <m>K</m>:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow> K \amp = 135d - 300 </mrow>
                                    <mrow> K + 300 \amp = 135d </mrow>
                                    <mrow> d(K) \amp = \frac{K + 300}{135} </mrow>
                                </md>
                                This inverse function represents the number of US Dollars originally exchanged, given a final payout amount in Kenyan Shillings.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Substitute <m>K = 26700</m> into the inverse function:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>d(26700) = \frac{26700 + 300}{135} = \frac{27000}{135} = 200</mrow>
                                </md>
                                The tourist exchanged <m>200</m> USD.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-functions-mombasa-tides">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The depth of water at the Kilindini Harbour in Mombasa fluctuates sinusoidally due to ocean tides. On a specific day, high tide occurs at 3:00 AM (<m>t = 3</m>) with a water depth of 13 meters. The subsequent low tide occurs at 9:00 AM (<m>t = 9</m>) with a water depth of 5 meters. Let <m>t</m> represent the number of hours after midnight.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Determine the amplitude, vertical shift (midline), and period of the tidal function.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Write a cosine function <m>D(t)</m> that models the water depth over time.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Calculate the depth of the water at 1:00 PM (<m>t = 13</m>).
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Amplitude = <m>4</m>, Vertical Shift = <m>9</m>, Period = <m>12</m> hours</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>D(t) = 4\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}(t - 3)\right) + 9</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>11</m> meters</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The amplitude is half the difference between the maximum and minimum depths: <m>\frac{13 - 5}{2} = 4</m>.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                The vertical shift (midline) is the average of the maximum and minimum depths: <m>\frac{13 + 5}{2} = 9</m>.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                A full tidal cycle goes from high tide to high tide. Since high to low takes 6 hours (from 3:00 AM to 9:00 AM), a full period is <m>2 \times 6 = 12</m> hours.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The standard cosine function is <m>D(t) = A\cos(B(t - C)) + k</m>. We know <m>A = 4</m> and <m>k = 9</m>.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                We find <m>B</m> using the period: <m>B = \frac{2\pi}{12} = \frac{\pi}{6}</m>.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                Since a maximum occurs at <m>t = 3</m>, the phase shift is <m>C = 3</m>. 
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                The function is: <m>D(t) = 4\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}(t - 3)\right) + 9</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Substitute <m>t = 13</m> into the model:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow> D(13) \amp = 4\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}(13 - 3)\right) + 9 </mrow>
                                    <mrow> \amp = 4\cos\left(\frac{10\pi}{6}\right) + 9 </mrow>
                                    <mrow> \amp = 4\cos\left(\frac{5\pi}{3}\right) + 9 </mrow>
                                </md>
                                Since <m>\cos\left(\frac{5\pi}{3}\right) = 0.5</m>:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>D(13) = 4(0.5) + 9 = 2 + 9 = 11 \text{ meters}</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-trig-kicc-tower">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The iconic Kenyatta International Convention Centre (KICC) tower in Nairobi stands at a height of approximately 105 meters. A student walking away from the base of the tower along City Hall Way looks up at the top of the tower. Let <m>x</m> represent the horizontal distance in meters from the base of the tower to the student, and let <m>\theta</m> represent the angle of elevation to the top of the tower in degrees.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-kicc-elevation-11">
                    <caption>Angle of elevation to the top of KICC</caption>
                    <image width="60%" xml:id="img-kicc-elevation-1">
                        <shortdescription>A right-angled triangle representing the KICC tower of height 105 meters, a horizontal ground distance x, and an angle of elevation theta.</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2]
                                % Draw ground and tower
                                \draw[thick, black] (0,0) -- (5,0) node[midway, below] {$x$ (Distance)};
                                \draw[thick, blue!70!black, line width=1.5pt] (0,0) -- (0,3.5) node[midway, left] {105 m (KICC)};
                                
                                % Draw line of sight
                                \draw[thick, dashed, red!80!black] (5,0) -- (0,3.5);
                                
                                % Right angle indicator
                                \draw (0,0.3) -- (0.3,0.3) -- (0.3,0);
                                
                                % Angle label
                                \draw (4.4,0) arc (180:145:0.6);
                                \node at (4.2,0.25) {$\theta$};
                                
                                % Decorative elements to symbolize the tower base and observer
                                \filldraw[black] (5,0) circle (1.5pt) node[below right] {Observer};
                                \filldraw[black] (0,3.5) circle (1.5pt) node[above left] {Top};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Write a trigonometric function that relates the angle <m>\theta</m> directly to the distance <m>x</m>. Then, rewrite it as an inverse trigonometric function where <m>\theta</m> is the output: <m>\theta(x)</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                State the practical domain and practical range of the function <m>\theta(x)</m> in this real-world context.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Calculate the exact angle of elevation <m>\theta</m> when the student stands exactly 105 meters away, and when they stand <m>105\sqrt{3}</m> meters (approximately 181.9 meters) away.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Find the algebraic expression for the inverse function, <m>x(\theta)</m>, and explain what this inverse function represents to the student.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\tan(\theta) = \frac{105}{x}</m> ; <m>\theta(x) = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{105}{x}\right)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Domain: <m>(0, \infty)</m> ; Range: <m>(0^\circ, 90^\circ)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>When <m>x = 105\text{ m}</m>, <m>\theta = 45^\circ</m>. When <m>x = 105\sqrt{3}\text{ m}</m>, <m>\theta = 30^\circ</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>x(\theta) = \frac{105}{\tan(\theta)}</m>. This function calculates the student's horizontal distance from the tower given a specific angle of elevation.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Using right-triangle trigonometry, the tangent of an angle is the ratio of the opposite side to the adjacent side:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>\tan(\theta) = \frac{\text{Opposite}}{\text{Adjacent}} = \frac{105}{x}</mrow>
                                </md>
                                To isolate <m>\theta</m>, we apply the inverse tangent function to both sides:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>\theta(x) = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{105}{x}\right)</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Practically, the distance <m>x</m> must be strictly greater than 0 meters as the student cannot occupy the exact same physical space as the base center of the tower. Thus, the practical domain is <m>x \in (0, \infty)</m>. 
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                As the student walks infinitely far away (<m>x \to \infty</m>), the angle approaches <m>0^\circ</m>. As the student gets infinitely close to the base (<m>x \to 0</m>), the angle approaches <m>90^\circ</m>. Therefore, the practical range is <m>\theta \in (0^\circ, 90^\circ)</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Substitute <m>x = 105</m> into our function:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>\theta(105) = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{105}{105}\right) = \tan^{-1}(1) = 45^\circ</mrow>
                                </md>
                                Substitute <m>x = 105\sqrt{3}</m> into our function:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>\theta(105\sqrt{3}) = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{105}{105\sqrt{3}}\right) = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\right) = 30^\circ</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                To determine the inverse function <m>x(\theta)</m>, we solve the primary equation back for <m>x</m>:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>\tan(\theta) \amp = \frac{105}{x}</mrow>
                                    <mrow>x \cdot \tan(\theta) \amp = 105</mrow>
                                    <mrow>x(\theta) \amp = \frac{105}{\tan(\theta)}</mrow>
                                </md>
                                In this context, the inverse function reverses the relationship: it inputs a measured angle of elevation <m>\theta</m> and outputs the exact horizontal distance <m>x</m> the student is standing from the KICC base.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <!-- [LP] Add information on how to differentiate  -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table>
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        For the activity, give learners a partly completed function rule for the mango situation, such as <m>C(k) = \_\_\_ \times k</m>, so they only need to fill in the rate. Focus the worked examples on the savings account and flare height questions first, since these use familiar linear and quadratic forms before moving to the more advanced examples involving square roots and trigonometry.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line>The core lesson activities as described above.</line>
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Ask learners to find their own real-world example of a function with a restricted practical domain, similar to the pendulum or escape velocity examples, and to justify the restriction in their own words. Learners can also explore the KICC tower exercise further by investigating how the angle of elevation changes if the tower height is doubled.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

